THOUGHT: NullAway reports `p` as @Nullable at `p.next = null;` in remove(). In the tail-removal branch, `p` is `curr.prev`, which can be null when the list has a single element. The code already checks `if (p != null)` before dereferencing, so the error likely refers to the middle-of-list case where `p` and `n` are used without null checks. To satisfy NullAway without changing behavior, I should add explicit null checks for `p` and `n` in the middle-of-list section. Then I must run the NullAway build to confirm the total error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '118,126c\ \ \ \ \ \ if (p != null && n != null) {
\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ p.next = n;
\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ n.prev = p;
\ \ \ \ \ \ }
\ \ \ }' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/PooledLinkedList.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway|error\(s\)" new_build_output.log | tail -n 20```

